\details{
\code{Period} is chosen as the target class because it preserves the calendar
components (years, months, days, hours, minutes, seconds) of an \href{https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc3339}{RFC 3339} / \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_8601}{ISO 8601} duration string separately,
rather than collapsing them into a single number of seconds. That fidelity
comes at a cost: years and months have no fixed length, so comparisons
(\code{min_value}, \code{max_value}, \code{allowed_values} in \code{\link[=stabilize_dur]{stabilize_dur()}}) use
lubridate's approximate, nominal lengths (a 365.25-day year, a 30.4375-day
month) and may not reflect the actual elapsed time implied by a specific
calendar date.

Character vectors must use the \href{https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc3339}{RFC 3339} \code{duration} format: \code{"P"}
followed by an optional number of years (\code{Y}), months (\code{M}), and days (\code{D}),
then an optional \code{"T"}-prefixed block of hours (\code{H}), minutes (\code{M}), and
seconds (\code{S}) (for example \code{"P3Y6M4DT12H30M5S"}, \code{"P23DT23H"}, or \code{"PT1M"});
or a stand-alone week form (\code{"P4W"}). The date/time form and the week form
cannot be mixed, fractional components are not permitted, and \code{"P"} or \code{"PT"}
alone (with no components) are not valid durations. \link[base:difftime]{base::difftime} values
(including \code{\link[hms:hms]{hms::hms()}}) are converted directly, preserving their unit.
Numeric and integer values are treated as a (fractional) number of seconds
and broken into day/hour/minute/second components, with no year or month
component, since a number of seconds cannot unambiguously imply a calendar
length.

\code{to_dur()} requires the \pkg{lubridate} package.
